{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 21, :comments ["<b>And Dishon and Etzer and Dishan.</b> According to the vocalization and the tradition of precise scrolls, this is the order of their reading: Dishon, Dishan, Dishon, Dishan, Dishan, Dishon, Dishan — and they are the days on which the scribe of the Torah takes them out (reads them): Dishon. And it begins on Shabbat: Dishon; on day seven, Dishan; on day one, Dishon; on day two, Dishan; on day three, Dishan; on day four, Dishon; on day five, Dishan; on day six. And do not wonder about ״ואלה בני דישן חמדן״ etc., which refers to the first Dishon, to say that it should have been vocalized Dishon — observe that in every place in Scripture, when the verse repeats what it has already mentioned, it changes either its spelling or its vocalization, such as Chronicles, which changes from what is written in the Torah. And the song of וידבר דוד לה׳ is different from how it is written in Psalms, and likewise Penuel / Peniel, Machuiel / Machiyel, and in Pharaoh's dream דקות / דקות / רקות, and many more without number. So too here: ״ואלה בני דישן חמדן״ — these are the sons of the first Dishon."], :book :genesis, :voice :chizkuni, :lang :en, :chapter 36, :notes {0 "The Aramaic phrase יומא דמפקי בהו ספרא דאורייתא is rendered literally; it refers to the days on which each name falls in the weekly Torah reading cycle, mapping the seven names to the seven days of the week."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T15:07:34.248094404Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}